<p class="hero-intro">
    The 2026 NFL Combine produced one of the fastest draft classes ever recorded — seven different position groups set combine-history speed records, and 22 players broke 4.40 in the 40-yard dash. Now those numbers get tested for real. Here's who to watch as training camps open across the league.
  </p>
</div><div class="article-wrap">
<div class="content">

<div class="callout" style="background:var(--surface);border-color:var(--accent);">
<div class="callout-icon">⚡</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Mississippi State WR <strong>Brenen Thompson</strong> ran the fastest official 40 at the 2026 combine, clocking <strong>4.26 seconds</strong>. Twenty-two players broke 4.40, and seven position groups — including linemen on both sides of the ball — posted their fastest average 40 times in combine history. Training camp is the first real test of whether that testing speed translates to football speed.</div>
</div>

<h2>A Historically Fast Combine Class</h2>
<p>Every position group at the 2026 NFL Combine ran faster, on average, than any combine class before it. Defensive backs and wide receivers tied for the fastest group average at <strong>4.44 seconds</strong>, with running backs close behind at 4.45. Even the trench positions got faster — offensive and defensive linemen both posted their best average 40 times on record.</p>
<p>Quarterbacks were part of the story too, averaging <strong>4.60 seconds</strong> in the 40 — the second-fastest average for the position in combine history, a reflection of how much mobility now factors into modern quarterback evaluation.</p>
