<h2>Rookies Worth Watching Once Pads Come On</h2>
<p><strong>Mike Washington Jr. (RB, Arkansas)</strong> — His 4.33 gave him what evaluators called a 99th-percentile weight-adjusted 40 time among drafted NFL running backs, an elite ratio of size to speed. Camp will show whether that translates to consistent explosiveness between the tackles, not just a fast forty-yard sprint in shorts.</p>
<p><strong>Jeremiyah Love (RB)</strong> — Already viewed as a likely top-10 pick before the combine, Love backed it up with a blazing 4.36, reinforcing his status as one of the first skill-position players off the board. His camp reps will be closely tracked as he transitions from a lead college back to an NFL workload.</p>
<p><strong>Kenyon Sadiq (TE, Oregon)</strong> — Not on the top-10 overall list, but his 4.39 was the fastest 40 ever run by a tight end at the combine. That kind of speed at the position tends to create immediate mismatches once real coverage is involved — see our full breakdown of <a href="/kenyon-sadiq-eli-stowers-combine-vertical-jump">Sadiq's historic combine performance</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Toriano Pride Jr. and Robert Spears-Jennings (DB)</strong> — Both ran identical 4.32s, and both play positions where straight-line speed has to hold up against NFL route-runners rather than a stopwatch. Camp one-on-ones will be the real test.</p>

<h2>Why Combine Speed Doesn't Always Show Up in Camp</h2>
<p>A fast 40 time is measured in a straight line, with no pads, no opponent, and no football in hand. Training camp introduces all three. Some combine standouts translate their testing speed directly onto the field — their acceleration and top-end speed show up in routes, angles, and closing bursts almost immediately. Others need a full camp, or even a rookie season, to convert raw sprint speed into functional football speed.</p>
<p>That gap is part of why scouts and coaches treat the combine as a data point rather than a verdict. A 4.26 gets a player noticed. What he does with it once someone is trying to tackle him is a separate question entirely — and it's the one training camp exists to answer.</p>

